consiSt("il",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Supreme Court of Illinois","d":"Court of last resort. Writes the Supreme Court Rules, which govern civil and appellate procedure alongside the Code of Civil Procedure, and controls bar admission and lawyer discipline (through the Attorney Registration and Disciplinary Commission). Hears most cases by discretionary petition for leave to appeal (Rule 315), and takes direct appeals when a circuit court holds a statute invalid (Rule 302(a))."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"Appellate Court of Illinois (five districts)","d":"Five districts sitting in Chicago (First), Elgin (Second), Ottawa (Third), Springfield (Fourth), and Mount Vernon (Fifth), deciding in three-judge panels. A five-judge Workers’ Compensation Commission division reviews those cases. Hears appeals of right from final circuit court judgments and interlocutory appeals under Rules 306–308 (Rules 22, 301)."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"Circuit Courts (judicial circuits)","d":"Single, unified general-jurisdiction trial court organized by judicial circuits and counties, staffed by circuit judges and associate judges (Rule 295). Cases are designated by division in the caption, e.g., ‘at law,’ ‘in chancery,’ ‘small claim’ (Rule 132)."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"No separate limited-jurisdiction courts","d":"Small claims (money claims up to $10,000) are a track within the circuit court with relaxed procedure (Rules 281–288). Circuits approved by the Supreme Court run court-annexed mandatory arbitration for money claims under a limit set for each circuit (Rules 86–95)."}],"slapp":[{"t":"Statute and scope: Citizen Participation Act (amended 2025)","d":"Illinois’s anti-SLAPP law is the Citizen Participation Act, 735 ILCS 110. It applies to any motion to dispose of a claim that is based on, relates to, or responds to the movant’s acts in furtherance of petition, speech, association, or participation in government, including freedom of the press. ‘Motion’ covers motions to dismiss, for summary judgment, or to strike, and ‘claim’ includes counterclaims and cross-claims.\nSuch acts are immune from liability whatever the intent, unless they were not genuinely aimed at procuring favorable government action, result, or outcome.\nThe 2025 amendments (P.A. 104-431, eff. Aug. 21, 2025) say the claim need not solely pertain to the movant’s protected rights and the Act applies regardless of the plaintiff’s motives. They also add press language, a stay, and new fee terms, and direct broad construction. Under §32 those changes apply only to actions commenced on or after January 1, 2026. Older actions are governed by the prior text, which was not collected.","c":"735 ILCS 110/10, 110/15, 110/5, 110/32","v":true},{"t":"Procedure: 90-day hearing, automatic stay, discovery suspended","d":"A hearing and decision must occur within 90 days after notice of the motion is given to the respondent. Discovery is suspended pending decision, except discovery the court allows for good cause on whether the movant’s acts are immunized or in furtherance of immunized acts.\nFor actions commenced on or after Jan. 1, 2026, filing the motion stays all other proceedings between the moving and responding parties, including discovery and pending motions. On motion, the court may stay related proceedings involving other parties. The stay lasts until the ruling and the time to appeal expire.\nExceptions: the plaintiff may still voluntarily dismiss; a fee motion is not stayed; and for good cause the court may hear unrelated motions or an injunction against an imminent public health or safety threat.","c":"735 ILCS 110/20(a), (b); 110/17(a)–(g)","v":true},{"t":"Standard: plaintiff must show clear and convincing evidence","d":"The court must grant the motion and dismiss unless the responding party produces clear and convincing evidence that the movant’s acts are not immunized, or are not in furtherance of immunized acts. The burden sits on the plaintiff (the responding party) at a heightened standard.","c":"735 ILCS 110/20(c)","v":true},{"t":"Fees: mandatory for a winning movant","d":"The court must award a prevailing movant reasonable attorney’s fees and costs incurred in connection with the motion. A prevailing respondent gets fees only if the court finds the motion frivolous or filed solely to delay.","c":"735 ILCS 110/25","v":true},{"t":"Appeal: denial reviewable by petition within 30 days","d":"An order denying a motion to dispose under the Act may be reviewed by petition for leave to appeal under Supreme Court Rule 306(a)(9), filed with a supporting record within 30 days. The appellate court must expedite any appeal from a denial, or from the trial court’s failure to rule within 90 days. Once a party appeals a ruling on the motion, all proceedings between all parties are stayed until the appeal ends (actions from 2026 on).\nA grant that ends the case is appealed as a final judgment (Rule 303). A grant disposing of fewer than all claims or parties needs a Rule 304(a) finding.\nNote: §17(b) refers to the time ‘under Section 20 to appeal,’ but §20 sets no appeal deadline, so use Rule 306’s 30 days.","c":"Ill. S. Ct. R. 306(a)(9), (c); 735 ILCS 110/20(a), 110/17(b)–(c)","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Rule 707: out-of-state lawyers appear by filing a Statement, not by motion","d":"An eligible out-of-state attorney may appear in an Illinois court case, court-annexed ADR, or an Illinois agency proceeding without any court order. File a verified Statement, and an active Illinois attorney must file an appearance in association.\nServe the Statement on the ARDC Administrator, Illinois co-counsel, the client, and all parties. It must list offices and contact details; parties represented; every Rule 707 appearance that calendar year; all admissions with bar numbers and good-standing certificates (except federal); any Illinois presence; submission to Illinois discipline; an undertaking to follow Illinois rules; and Illinois counsel’s ARDC number. Eligibility requires unrestricted admission elsewhere (U.S. or foreign), no disciplinary bar, and no unlimited or conditional Illinois admission.","c":"Ill. S. Ct. R. 707(a)–(d)","v":true},{"t":"Fee: $250 per proceeding, plus annual ARDC registration","d":"Pay a nonrefundable $250 per proceeding to the ARDC Administrator when serving the Statement. Exempt: lawyers appointed for indigent parties, nonprofit legal-services lawyers in civil cases for program clients, Rule 718 disaster-relief lawyers, and U.S. Department of Justice lawyers representing the United States.\nThe lawyer must also register with the ARDC within 30 days of filing the Statement, if not already registered, and pay the Rule 756 registration fee for each year with a Rule 707 appearance of record.","c":"R. 707(f), (h)","v":true},{"t":"Volume limit: no more than five other proceedings a year","d":"Eligibility is lost if the attorney has already appeared in more than five other proceedings under Rule 707 in the calendar year the Statement is filed. An appeal or review of a proceeding before a different tribunal counts as a separate proceeding and needs its own Statement and fee.","c":"R. 707(b)(2), (c)","v":true},{"t":"Local counsel must actively participate; permission can be terminated","d":"The Illinois lawyer must stay of record and actively participate (Rule 5.5(c)(1)). The Supreme Court, the chief judge, or the trial court may terminate permission for lost qualifications, misconduct, inaccurate Statements, unpaid fees, failure to respond to the Administrator, or the absence of active local counsel.\nNew information about admissions, Illinois presence, or local counsel, plus any discipline or conviction and the end of the representation, must be reported in writing to the Administrator within 30 days.","c":"R. 707(e), (i); Ill. R. Prof’l Conduct 5.5(c)(1)","v":true},{"t":"Temporary practice without a proceeding: Rule 5.5","d":"A lawyer admitted elsewhere in the U.S. and not suspended may provide temporary services in Illinois in association with an actively participating Illinois lawyer; for a pending or expected proceeding; in ADR reasonably related to home-state practice when the forum does not require pro hac vice; or for other matters arising from home-state practice. Establishing a systematic and continuous presence or holding out as Illinois-admitted is prohibited.","c":"Ill. R. Prof’l Conduct 5.5(b), (c), (d)","v":true},{"t":"Admission on motion and other limited admissions","d":"Admission on motion (Rule 705) requires at least 3 years’ licensure and active, continuous, lawful practice for 3 of the 5 years before applying. That means at least 80 hours a month and 1,000 hours a year during 36 of the last 60 months. Lawyers licensed fewer than 15 years must have passed the MPRE, and character-and-fitness certification and good standing everywhere are required.\nOther routes include a transferred UBE score (Rule 704A), house counsel (Rule 716), legal service program lawyers (Rule 717), and military spouses (Rule 719). Only Rules 705 and 707 were read in detail.","c":"Ill. S. Ct. R. 705(a)–(h); R. 704A, 716, 717, 719","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope appearances are expressly allowed","d":"Under a written limited-scope agreement (Rule 1.2(c)), a lawyer may file a statewide-form Notice of Limited Scope Appearance for specific aspects of a civil case, and may file more than one. When done, the lawyer withdraws by filing a Notice of Completion, in open court or by serving it with an Objection form. If the client objects within 21 days, withdrawal is denied only on clear and convincing proof the work is incomplete.\nWhile the limited appearance lasts, documents must be served on both lawyer and client. A lawyer may also ghostwrite a pro se filing without appearing at all.","c":"R. 13(c)(6)–(7); R. 11(f); R. 137(e)","v":true},{"t":"E-filing is mandatory statewide; e-mail service is the default","d":"All civil documents in all courts must be e-filed through a Supreme Court-approved system unless exempt (original wills cannot be e-filed). A filing is timely if submitted before midnight in the court’s time zone on the due date. If the clerk rejects a filing, or the system fails, move within 5 court days to have the corrected document deemed filed on the original date.\nAttorneys must designate a primary e-mail address (and up to two secondary ones) on every filing, and documents are served by e-mail or through the e-filing provider. Whether out-of-state Rule 707 lawyers can hold their own e-filing accounts is not addressed in the rules collected.","c":"R. 9(a), (c), (f); R. 11(b)–(c); R. 131(d)(1)","v":true},{"t":"Attorney-issued subpoenas need an Illinois-admitted lawyer","d":"Deposition subpoenas may be issued by the clerk or by ‘an attorney admitted to practice in the State of Illinois who is currently counsel of record.’ The rule does not say whether a Rule 707 lawyer qualifies, so have Illinois co-counsel or the clerk issue them. Subpoenas for out-of-state cases go through Rule 17 (Uniform Interstate Depositions and Discovery Act).","c":"R. 204(a)(1), (b)(2)","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: Illinois Rules of Professional Conduct of 2010","d":"Article VIII of the Supreme Court Rules, adopted July 1, 2009, effective January 1, 2010, and amended many times since (e.g., Rule 1.6 in 2025, Rule 8.4 in 2024). It follows the ABA Model Rules numbering with the Illinois differences below. Discipline is handled by the ARDC under Rules 751–780.","c":"Ill. S. Ct. Rules, art. VIII; R. 751","v":true},{"t":"Confidentiality: disclosure to prevent death or serious harm is mandatory","d":"Rule 1.6(c) requires (not just permits) disclosure to the extent reasonably necessary to prevent reasonably certain death or substantial bodily harm. Rule 1.6(b)(1) also permits disclosure to prevent the client from committing any crime, broader than the ABA’s permissive exceptions. Information shared in approved lawyers’ assistance, intermediary, or lawyer-connection (ICS) programs is treated as confidential client information.","c":"Ill. R. Prof’l Conduct 1.6(b)(1), (c), (d)","v":true},{"t":"Fees: nonrefundable retainers banned; detailed retainer types","d":"Nonrefundable fees and retainers are prohibited, as are agreements restricting the right to fire the lawyer or to get a refund. Rule 1.5(d) defines fixed fees (not deposited in trust), engagement retainers, security retainers (client property; must go in trust), and special purpose retainers (writing required, with five listed disclosures and an offer of a security retainer).\nFee splitting with another firm is allowed in proportion to work, or for a referral if each lawyer assumes joint financial responsibility, with the client’s written-confirmed agreement to each share.","c":"Ill. R. Prof’l Conduct 1.5(c), (d), (f)","v":true},{"t":"Duty to report is narrower, plus self-reporting of outside discipline","d":"A lawyer must report another lawyer only for known criminal acts reflecting on fitness (8.4(b)) or dishonesty, fraud, deceit, or misrepresentation (8.4(c)), not for every violation raising a substantial question of fitness. A lawyer disciplined, or transferred to disability inactive status, by any body other than the Illinois ARDC must report it to the ARDC.","c":"Ill. R. Prof’l Conduct 8.3(a), (d)","v":true},{"t":"Misconduct: extra Illinois prohibitions","d":"Illinois adds these items to Rule 8.4:\n• threatening criminal or disciplinary charges to gain an advantage in a civil matter (8.4(g));\n• agreements limiting a client’s right to complain to the ARDC (8.4(h));\n• bad-faith avoidance of government-guaranteed student loans (8.4(i));\n• gifts to judges beyond what the Code of Judicial Conduct allows, and judicial campaign contributions other than by check to a political committee (8.4(f));\n• specific limits on lawyer-legislators (8.4(k)).\nRule 8.4(j) bars harassment or discrimination in the practice of law on listed bases, including socioeconomic status, that the lawyer knows or reasonably should know about.","c":"Ill. R. Prof’l Conduct 8.4(f)–(k)","v":true},{"t":"No Rule 6.1 pro bono rule; lateral screening","d":"Illinois has no counterpart to ABA Model Rule 6.1. The Preamble says pro bono is a responsibility but not a disciplinary standard. Lateral-hire conflicts under Rule 1.9 can be cured by timely screening and no fee share (1.10(e)); the Illinois text has no written-notice-to-former-client requirement.","c":"Ill. R. Prof’l Conduct, Preamble cmt. [6B]; R. 1.10(e)","v":true}],"style":"State of Illinois","home":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"Requests to admit: 28 days, sworn, or the facts are deemed true","d":"Rule 216 requests are admitted unless within 28 days you serve a sworn denial (or detailed reasons) or written objections. An unsworn lawyer letter does not count. The request must be a separate document with the boldface warning; up to 30 requests, subparts counted.","c":"Ill. S. Ct. R. 216(c), (f), (g)"},{"t":"Filing anything before a 2-301 motion waives personal jurisdiction","d":"An objection to personal jurisdiction must come before any other pleading or motion; filing something else first waives it prospectively. The only exceptions are an extension motion, or a 2-1301/2-1401 motion (then object within 60 days of its disposition, or combine the two). Losing the objection and then participating also waives error unless the ground is non-amenability to process.","c":"735 ILCS 5/2-301(a), (a-6), (c)"},{"t":"Substitution of judge as of right disappears after a substantial ruling","d":"You get one no-cause substitution, but only if you move before trial or hearing begins and before the judge rules on any substantial issue. Decide on day one.","c":"735 ILCS 5/2-1001(a)(2)(ii)"},{"t":"Partial judgments are not appealable without a 304(a) finding","d":"An order resolving fewer than all claims or parties is neither appealable nor enforceable without an express written finding of ‘no just reason for delaying either enforcement or appeal or both.’ With the finding, the 30-day clock starts on the finding date, so waiting can forfeit the appeal. A pending Rule 137 claim also blocks finality.","c":"Ill. S. Ct. R. 304(a); R. 303(a)(1)"},{"t":"Only one postjudgment motion tolls; reconsideration of the ruling does not","d":"Each party gets one postjudgment motion per final judgment within 30 days. A motion to reconsider the denial of a postjudgment motion does not extend the 30 days to appeal.","c":"Ill. S. Ct. R. 274; R. 303(a)(2); 735 ILCS 5/2-1202(c), 2-1203(a)"},{"t":"Witness disclosure limits trial testimony","d":"On direct examination a witness may testify only to what was disclosed in Rule 213(f) answers or in a discovery deposition. Controlled experts need full opinions, bases, qualifications, and reports disclosed, and evidence-deposition material not previously disclosed is excluded on objection.","c":"Ill. S. Ct. R. 213(f), (g)"},{"t":"Discovery vs. evidence depositions","d":"Unless the notice says ‘evidence deposition,’ it is a discovery deposition only, usable at trial essentially for impeachment, admissions, or affidavit purposes. Discovery depositions are capped at 3 hours, and nonparty treating physicians cannot be deposed without agreement plus consent or a court-ordered subpoena.","c":"Ill. S. Ct. R. 202, 206(d), 212(a), 204(c)"},{"t":"Jury demand timing","d":"A plaintiff must file a jury demand when the action is commenced, and a defendant no later than filing the answer, or the jury is waived. Small claims demands are due at filing (plaintiff) or by the appearance date (defendant).","c":"735 ILCS 5/2-1105(a); Ill. S. Ct. R. 285"},{"t":"Silence is admission in an Illinois answer","d":"Every allegation except damages that is not explicitly denied is admitted. Pleading insufficient knowledge requires an affidavit. ‘Neither admits nor denies’ answers are risky.","c":"735 ILCS 5/2-610(b)"},{"t":"Serve diligently, even within the limitations period","d":"A defendant may be dismissed for lack of reasonable diligence in service; if the lack of diligence occurs after limitations runs, the dismissal is with prejudice. Courts weigh diligence in earlier dismissed or refiled cases too.","c":"Ill. S. Ct. R. 103(b)"},{"t":"Combined motions must be split into labeled parts","d":"Combining 2-615, 2-619, and 2-1005 arguments in one undifferentiated motion violates 2-619.1. Each part must name its section and grounds, and 2-619/2-1005 affidavits must meet Rule 191(a).","c":"735 ILCS 5/2-619.1; Ill. S. Ct. R. 191(a)"},{"t":"Rule 707 limits: five proceedings a year, and the appeal is a new one","d":"An out-of-state lawyer becomes ineligible after more than five other Rule 707 appearances in a calendar year. Each appeal or agency review counts as a separate proceeding requiring a new Statement and a $250 fee. Trial counsel’s appearance ends when the notice of appeal is filed.","c":"Ill. S. Ct. R. 707(b)(2), (c), (f); R. 13(c)(8)"}],"judge":[{"t":"One substitution of judge as of right, but only before a substantial ruling","d":"Each party gets one substitution of judge without cause, by motion. It must be granted if presented before trial or hearing begins and before the judge has ruled on any substantial issue (or by consent).\nA party who had not yet appeared (and was not in default) is not barred by rulings made before its appearance. Move at the first opportunity: once the judge rules on something substantial, the right is gone.","c":"735 ILCS 5/2-1001(a)(2)","v":true},{"t":"Substitution for cause: verified petition heard by another judge","d":"There is no limit on for-cause petitions. The petition must state the specific cause and be verified by the applicant’s affidavit, and a different judge hears it as soon as possible. The challenged judge may submit an affidavit but need not testify. If allowed, the case is reassigned; if denied, it returns to the same judge.\nA judge who is a party, interested, a material witness, related to a party, or former counsel in the matter may be substituted with or without a request.","c":"735 ILCS 5/2-1001(a)(1), (a)(3), (b)–(c)","v":true},{"t":"Judicial disqualification under the 2023 Code of Judicial Conduct","d":"Separately, a judge must disqualify whenever impartiality might reasonably be questioned. Examples include personal bias or knowledge of disputed facts; a spouse, domestic partner, or relative within the third degree as party, lawyer, interested person, or likely material witness; or a family economic interest in the matter.","c":"Ill. Code of Judicial Conduct of 2023, Rule 2.11(A) (Ill. S. Ct. Rules, art. XI)","v":true},{"t":"Small claims: substitution motion allowed without leave","d":"In small claims cases most motions need leave of court, but motions under 2-619 and 2-1001 (substitution of judge) may be filed without it.","c":"Ill. S. Ct. R. 287(b)","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"Ill. Const. art. I, § 4: not established here","d":"The Illinois Constitution has its own free speech and press guarantee, but the collected texts do not include the Constitution or case law. Whether Illinois courts read it more broadly than the First Amendment was not checked.","c":"Ill. Const. 1970, art. I, § 4 (not in collected texts)","v":false},{"t":"Statutory protection: Citizen Participation Act covers the press","d":"The amended Citizen Participation Act expressly protects the press when it reports, opines, or investigates on matters of public concern. It also calls for the ‘utmost protection’ of petition, speech, association, and government participation, and says the Act should be construed broadly.","c":"735 ILCS 110/5, 110/15","v":true}],"other":[{"t":"Key Code of Civil Procedure motions: 2-615, 2-619, 2-619.1","d":"Section 2-615 motions attack legal sufficiency on the face of the pleading and must specify the defects; they also cover motions to strike, make more definite, and for judgment on the pleadings. Section 2-619 motions, filed within the time to plead, raise listed defenses outside the pleading (lack of subject-matter jurisdiction, capacity, another pending action, prior judgment, limitations, release, statute of frauds, disability, ‘other affirmative matter’), supported by affidavit when not apparent on the face.\nUnder 2-619.1, 2-615, 2-619, and 2-1005 motions may be combined, but only in separately labeled parts.","c":"735 ILCS 5/2-615, 2-619, 2-619.1","v":true},{"t":"Pleadings: specific denials, attach the instrument","d":"Every allegation not explicitly denied is admitted, except damages, unless the answer claims lack of knowledge supported by an affidavit. A claim or defense founded on a written instrument must attach it (or recite it) unless an affidavit shows it is not accessible.","c":"735 ILCS 5/2-610(b), 2-606","v":true},{"t":"Postjudgment motions and relief from judgments","d":"Post-trial motions in jury cases (single motion) and motions after judgment in nonjury cases are due within 30 days of judgment and stay enforcement. A 2-1203 motion does not stay an injunction or declaratory judgment without a separate order. After 30 days, relief requires a 2-1401 petition, generally within 2 years.","c":"735 ILCS 5/2-1202, 2-1203, 2-1401","v":true},{"t":"Rule 23 orders can be cited (persuasively) since 2021","d":"Nonprecedential Appellate Court Rule 23(b) orders entered on or after January 1, 2021 may be cited for persuasive purposes; older ones and summary orders may be cited only for res judicata, collateral estoppel, double jeopardy, or law of the case.","c":"Ill. S. Ct. R. 23(e)(1)","v":true},{"t":"Cases up to $50,000: affidavit, mandatory disclosures, limited discovery","d":"Every money-damages complaint must attach an affidavit stating whether damages exceed $50,000. If not, Rule 222 applies: written initial disclosures (within 120 days after the responsive pleading absent a local rule) with exclusion of undisclosed evidence, 30 interrogatories, 3-hour party/expert depositions, and a $50,000 cap on any judgment.","c":"Ill. S. Ct. R. 222(a)–(g)","v":true},{"t":"Remote appearances are built into the rules","d":"Remote appearance by phone or video is generally equivalent to in-person under Rule 45. Remote testimony needs good cause and safeguards (Rule 241), and remote depositions are allowed on notice (Rule 206(h)).","c":"Ill. S. Ct. R. 45(a), 241, 206(h)","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Illinois Supreme Court Rules, Article I (Rules 9, 11–13, 23) and Article III (Civil Appeals Rules 301–384), official compilation “Current October 21, 2026”; Code of Civil Procedure 735 ILCS 5/2-1202, 2-1203, 2-1401 (ILGA database, fetched November 10, 2026); Citizen Participation Act 735 ILCS 110/17, 110/20 (as amended by P.A. 104-431). Summaries are Consi’s.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in Illinois: 30 days, one notice, and a Supreme Court rule for almost everything","d":"Civil appeals go from the circuit court to the Appellate Court (five districts) and then, almost always by discretionary petition for leave to appeal, to the Illinois Supreme Court. The procedure is set by Supreme Court Rules 301–384, not by a statute or a separate rules of appellate procedure. Every final judgment is appealable as of right, and the only jurisdictional step is a timely notice of appeal filed in the circuit court within 30 days (Rules 301, 303).\nThe traps are mostly about finality: partial judgments need a Rule 304(a) finding, only one postjudgment motion tolls the clock, and a pending Rule 137 sanctions claim keeps the judgment from being final. Interlocutory review exists as of right for injunctions and receivers (Rule 307), by permission for a list of orders that includes forum non conveniens, personal jurisdiction, class certification, and Citizen Participation Act denials (Rule 306), and by certified question (Rule 308)."},"groups":[["Starting the appeal",[{"t":"Final judgments are appealable as of right; notice of appeal is the only jurisdictional step","d":"Every final judgment of a circuit court in a civil case is appealable as of right. The appeal begins by filing a notice of appeal in the circuit court; no other step is jurisdictional, and the appeal is a continuation of the proceeding. The circuit clerk forwards the notice to the reviewing court within 5 days.","c":"Ill. S. Ct. R. 301; R. 303(a)(4)"},{"t":"30 days from judgment, or from the order deciding the last postjudgment motion","d":"File the notice within 30 days after entry of the final judgment or, if a timely postjudgment motion is filed, within 30 days after entry of the order disposing of the last pending postjudgment motion. A notice filed after the decision is announced but before entry counts as filed on entry. Any attorney may file the notice, even one who has not appeared in the circuit court.","c":"R. 303(a)(1)"},{"t":"When a judgment is ‘entered’","d":"If the judge requires a signed written judgment (or a local rule requires a draft order), the judgment is final only when the signed judgment is filed; otherwise it is entered when entered of record.","c":"R. 272"},{"t":"Contents, amendment, and service of the notice","d":"Caption ‘Appeal to the ___ Court / From the Circuit Court of ___,’ designate it as notice of appeal, joining prior appeal, separate appeal, or cross-appeal, and specify the judgment or parts appealed and relief sought. It may be amended without leave within the original 30 days; later only by motion in the reviewing court. Within 7 days file a notice of filing in the reviewing court and serve the notice on every party, with proof of service.","c":"R. 303(b), (c)"},{"t":"Late notice of appeal: one extra 30-day window","d":"Within 30 days after the time to appeal expires, a party may move in the reviewing court for leave to file a late notice, showing reasonable excuse and attaching the proposed notice and filing fee. Other parties then have 10 days from the order to join, appeal separately, or cross-appeal.","c":"R. 303(d)"},{"t":"Cross-appeals and separate appeals","d":"After a timely notice is served, any other party may join, appeal separately, or cross-appeal within 10 days after service of the notice, or within 30 days after the judgment or the order deciding the last postjudgment motion, whichever is later.","c":"R. 303(a)(3)"},{"t":"Direct appeals to the Supreme Court","d":"Appeals go straight to the Supreme Court when a circuit court holds a U.S. or Illinois statute invalid (not counting federal preemption rulings), with the Rule 18 findings attached to the notice. The Supreme Court may also take a case directly when the public interest requires prompt decision.","c":"R. 302(a), (b); R. 303(b)(3)"}]],["Finality and postjudgment motions",[{"t":"Partial judgments need a Rule 304(a) finding","d":"When there are multiple parties or claims, a judgment disposing of fewer than all is appealable only if the trial court makes an express written finding that there is no just reason to delay enforcement or appeal or both. The finding’s entry date starts the 30 days. Without it, the order is not appealable or enforceable and may be revised anytime.","c":"R. 304(a)"},{"t":"Orders appealable without a finding","d":"Rule 304(b) lists orders immediately appealable without a 304(a) finding, under the Rule 303 timing: estate and guardianship orders finally determining a right or status, receivership and similar orders, rulings on 2-1401 petitions, final orders in 2-1402 citation proceedings, contempt orders imposing a penalty, and custody or parental-responsibility judgments.","c":"R. 304(b)(1)–(6)"},{"t":"Pending sanctions claim blocks finality","d":"A judgment is not final and appealable while a Rule 137 claim remains pending unless the court enters a Rule 304(a) finding. A pending Rule 137 claim does not extend the time for postjudgment motions.","c":"R. 303(a)(1); R. 274"},{"t":"Postjudgment motions: 30 days, and only one","d":"Jury cases: all post-trial relief (judgment n.o.v., new trial, renewed directed verdict) goes in a single post-trial motion within 30 days after judgment, stating the grounds specifically; failing to request a new trial waives it. Nonjury cases: a motion to rehear, retry, modify, or vacate within 30 days. Extensions must be granted within the 30 days. Each party gets one postjudgment motion per final judgment, and a timely one tolls the appeal time.","c":"735 ILCS 5/2-1202(b), (c), (e); 735 ILCS 5/2-1203(a); R. 274"},{"t":"Motions to reconsider a postjudgment ruling do not toll","d":"No request to reconsider a ruling on a postjudgment motion tolls the time to appeal. A notice filed while a timely postjudgment motion is pending becomes effective when the motion is decided, but to challenge the ruling on the motion or an amended judgment you must file a new or amended notice within 30 days of that order.","c":"R. 303(a)(2)"},{"t":"After 30 days: section 2-1401 petition","d":"Relief from a final judgment after 30 days requires a petition under 2-1401, filed in the same case (but a new proceeding), supported by affidavit, generally within 2 years after the judgment (excluding periods of disability, duress, or fraudulent concealment). The ruling is appealable under Rule 304(b)(3).","c":"735 ILCS 5/2-1401(a)–(c); R. 304(b)(3); R. 106"}]],["Interlocutory appeals",[{"t":"As of right: injunctions, receivers, and similar orders (Rule 307)","d":"Orders granting, modifying, refusing, dissolving, or refusing to dissolve an injunction; appointing or refusing a receiver; placing a mortgagee in possession; and several others may be appealed as of right. File a ‘Notice of Interlocutory Appeal’ and the Rule 328 supporting record within 30 days of the order. If the order was entered ex parte, first move to vacate; the 30 days run from denial or from 7 days after presentment if the court does not act. Briefs follow a 7-7-7 day schedule.","c":"R. 307(a)–(c)"},{"t":"Temporary restraining orders: 2 days","d":"Review of a TRO ruling is by petition in the Appellate Court, plus a notice of interlocutory appeal in the circuit court, both within 2 days of the order, with a supporting record. Memoranda are limited to 15 pages or 4,500 words; the response is due 2 days later; no replies or extensions; the court decides within 5 business days without argument.","c":"R. 307(d)"},{"t":"By permission: petition for leave to appeal (Rule 306)","d":"Listed orders may be reviewed by petition: new-trial grants; forum non conveniens rulings; denial of a motion to dismiss for lack of personal jurisdiction; certain venue transfers; custody orders; remands to an agency; orders disqualifying counsel; class certification rulings; and denials of a motion to dispose under the Citizen Participation Act. File the petition and Rule 328 supporting record within 30 days of the order; answers are due 21 days after filing; the court decides within 30 days after briefing closes. Granting the petition stays the trial court.","c":"R. 306(a)(1)–(9), (c)"},{"t":"Certified questions (Rule 308)","d":"If the trial court certifies in writing that an interlocutory order involves a question of law with substantial ground for difference of opinion and that immediate appeal may materially advance the litigation, a party may apply to the Appellate Court within 30 days after the order or the certification, whichever is later. Answers are due 21 days after the application’s due date. The application does not stay the trial court unless ordered.","c":"R. 308(a)–(d), (f)"}]],["Stays and bonds",[{"t":"Money judgments: bond within the appeal time","d":"Enforcement of a money judgment is stayed if a timely notice of appeal is filed and an appeal bond or other approved security is presented, approved, and filed within the time to appeal (or extension). Security normally covers the judgment, costs, and anticipated interest; the court may approve less if full security is not reasonably available, with conditions against dissipating assets. Circuit court extensions may not total more than 45 days unless the parties stipulate.","c":"R. 305(a), (c)"},{"t":"Other judgments; reviewing-court stays","d":"Stays of nonmoney judgments and appealable orders are discretionary on terms, and a bond is required to protect an appellee’s property interest. Ask the circuit court first; a reviewing court motion must show that was impractical or unsuccessful and include a supporting record. Public bodies may be stayed without bond.","c":"R. 305(b), (d), (i)"},{"t":"No stay, no protection against third-party buyers","d":"If a stay is not perfected within the appeal time, reversal does not affect property rights acquired by nonparties after the judgment became final and before the stay.","c":"R. 305(k)"}]],["Docketing statement and record",[{"t":"Docketing statement in 14 days","d":"Appellants and cross-appellants file a docketing statement within 14 days after the notice of appeal (7 days for Rule 307 appeals; with the petition for Rule 306/308 appeals), with the filing fee and their written requests to the circuit clerk and court reporters for the record. The appellee may respond within 7 days.","c":"R. 312(a), (b)"},{"t":"Report of proceedings","d":"Within the docketing-statement time, ask court reporting personnel in writing for the transcript you want; the appellee may designate more within 7 days, and the appellant must then request it or move to have the appellee advance the cost. Including unnecessary matter can be taxed against the designating party.","c":"R. 323(a)"},{"t":"Record due 63 days after the notice of appeal","d":"The record on appeal must be filed within 63 days after the (last) notice of appeal, or 14 days after an extended transcript deadline. Extensions require a motion before the deadline or within 35 days after it with a reasonable excuse.","c":"R. 326"}]],["Briefs",[{"t":"Schedule: 35 / 35 / 14","d":"Appellant’s brief is due 35 days after the record is filed, appellee’s 35 days after the appellant’s due date, and the reply 14 days after the appellee’s due date. A cross-appellant files one combined appellee/cross-appellant brief; the appellant’s reply and cross-appeal answer is due 35 days later; the cross-reply 14 days after that. Extensions need a motion supported by affidavit or 1-109 certification.","c":"R. 343(a)–(c)"},{"t":"Length: 50 pages or 15,000 words; reply 20 pages or 6,000","d":"Opening and response briefs are limited to 50 pages or 15,000 words, replies to 20 pages or 6,000 words; cross-appeals get an extra 30 pages or 9,000 words. Covers, table of contents and points and authorities, certificates, and the Rule 342 appendix are excluded. A signed certificate of compliance is required. Motions for extra length are disfavored and due at least 10 days before the brief (5 for a reply), with an affidavit.","c":"R. 341(b), (c)"},{"t":"Format and colored covers","d":"Double-spaced 12-point text, 1½-inch left margin and 1-inch elsewhere, no condensed type. Even e-filed briefs use colored covers: appellant white, appellee light blue, reply light yellow, rehearing petition light green.","c":"R. 341(a), (d)"},{"t":"Points not argued are forfeited","d":"The appellant’s brief must state the standard of review for each issue, and the argument must contain contentions with citations to authority and the record. Points not argued are forfeited and cannot be raised in the reply, at oral argument, or on rehearing. The appendix must include the judgment, the notice of appeal, and a table of contents of the record.","c":"R. 341(h); R. 342"}]],["Decisions, rehearing, and mandate",[{"t":"Rule 23 orders: nonprecedential but citable since 2021","d":"Appellate decisions are full opinions or Rule 23 written or summary orders. Rule 23 orders are not precedential (except for res judicata, double jeopardy, collateral estoppel, or law of the case), but a Rule 23(b) order entered on or after January 1, 2021 may be cited for persuasive purposes.","c":"R. 23(a)–(c), (e)(1)"},{"t":"Rehearing: 21 days","d":"A petition for rehearing is due within 21 days after the judgment, limited to 27 pages or 8,100 words, stating points overlooked or misapprehended without reargument. No answer is allowed unless requested; an answer is due 21 days after request or grant and a reply 14 days later. Only one rehearing round in the Appellate Court.","c":"R. 367(a)–(e)"},{"t":"Mandate: no earlier than 35 days","d":"The mandate issues no earlier than 35 days after judgment (or after denial of rehearing) unless the court orders otherwise; a timely rehearing petition stays it.","c":"R. 368(a)"}]],["Review in the Illinois Supreme Court",[{"t":"Petition for leave to appeal: 35 days","d":"Review is discretionary, considering general importance, conflicts with the Supreme Court or another Appellate Court division, the need for supervisory authority, and finality. File within 35 days after the Appellate Court judgment, or after denial of a timely rehearing petition or entry of judgment on rehearing. For Rule 23 orders, a timely Rule 23(f) motion to publish restarts the 35 days. Extensions are allowed only in the most extreme and compelling circumstances.","c":"R. 315(a), (b)"},{"t":"Petition format and answer","d":"Petition and answer are each limited to 20 pages or 6,000 words, follow the brief rules, and include the Appellate Court decision in an appendix. An answer is optional, due 21 days after the petition deadline; no reply. If leave is granted, the appellant must within 14 days elect to stand on the petition or file a brief, due 35 days after leave was allowed.","c":"R. 315(c), (d), (f), (h)"},{"t":"Appeals as of right and cross-relief","d":"An appeal as of right lies when the Appellate Court holds a statute invalid or a constitutional question first arises from its decision, by a ‘Petition for Appeal as a Matter of Right’ following Rule 315 procedure (leave may be sought in the alternative). Any appellee or coparty may get relief warranted by the record without its own petition.","c":"R. 317; R. 318(a)"}]],["Filing, service, and fees",[{"t":"Mandatory e-filing; filed when received","d":"Civil documents, including in reviewing courts, must be e-filed through an approved system unless exempt. A filing is timely if submitted before midnight in the court’s time zone on the due date. In the reviewing court, documents count as filed when actually received by the clerk; only paper filers exempt from e-filing get the mailing date (within 3 business days). This rule also applies to postjudgment motions and notices of appeal in the trial court.","c":"R. 9(a), (f); R. 373"},{"t":"E-mail service and proof","d":"Serve documents by e-mail or through the e-filing provider to the addresses of record; service by e-mail is complete on transmission. Proof is the automated EFSP verification or a 1-109 certification.","c":"R. 11(c); R. 12(b), (c)"},{"t":"Reviewing court fees","d":"Appellants and petitioners pay a $50 filing fee; other parties pay $30 on appearance or first filing; a nonparty (including an amicus seeking leave) pays $30.","c":"R. 313(a)"},{"t":"Trial counsel’s appearance ends with the notice of appeal","d":"An attorney’s trial-court appearance ends when a notice of appeal is filed from the final judgment, or when the time to file it expires. An out-of-state lawyer appearing under Rule 707 needs a new Statement for the appeal, which is a separate proceeding.","c":"R. 13(c)(8); R. 707(c)"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal from final judgment","d":"30 days from entry of final judgment, or entry of order disposing of last pending timely postjudgment motion.\nJurisdictional. Filed in the circuit court.","c":"Ill. S. Ct. R. 303(a)(1)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of final judgment, or entry of order disposing of last pending timely postjudgment motion)","note":"Jurisdictional. Filed in the circuit court."}},{"t":"Motion for leave to file late notice of appeal","d":"30 days from expiration of the time to file the notice of appeal.\nFiled in the reviewing court with reasonable excuse, proposed notice, and fee.","c":"R. 303(d)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (expiration of the time to file the notice of appeal)","note":"Filed in the reviewing court with reasonable excuse, proposed notice, and fee."}},{"t":"Cross-appeal, separate appeal, or joining appeal","d":"10 days from service of another party’s timely notice of appeal (or 30 days from judgment / last postjudgment order, whichever is later).\nUse whichever period ends later.","c":"R. 303(a)(3)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (service of another party’s timely notice of appeal)","note":"Use whichever period ends later."}},{"t":"Serve notice of appeal and file notice of filing in reviewing court","d":"7 days from filing of notice of appeal.","c":"R. 303(c)","due":{"n":7,"from":"the trigger date (filing of notice of appeal)","note":""}},{"t":"Post-trial motion (jury case)","d":"30 days from entry of judgment or discharge of jury without verdict.\nSingle motion; extensions must be granted within the 30 days.","c":"735 ILCS 5/2-1202(c)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment or discharge of jury without verdict)","note":"Single motion; extensions must be granted within the 30 days."}},{"t":"Postjudgment motion (nonjury case)","d":"30 days from entry of judgment.\nOnly one postjudgment motion per judgment tolls the appeal time.","c":"735 ILCS 5/2-1203(a); R. 274","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Only one postjudgment motion per judgment tolls the appeal time."}},{"t":"Rule 137 sanctions motion","d":"30 days from entry of final judgment, or ruling on timely postjudgment motion.\nA pending Rule 137 claim keeps the judgment non-final absent a 304(a) finding.","c":"R. 137(b)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of final judgment, or ruling on timely postjudgment motion)","note":"A pending Rule 137 claim keeps the judgment non-final absent a 304(a) finding."}},{"t":"Section 2-1401 petition for relief from judgment","d":"730 days from entry of the judgment.\nStatute says 2 years; excludes disability, duress, and fraudulent concealment periods.","c":"735 ILCS 5/2-1401(c)","due":{"n":730,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the judgment)","note":"Statute says 2 years; excludes disability, duress, and fraudulent concealment periods."}},{"t":"Notice of interlocutory appeal as of right and Rule 328 supporting record","d":"30 days from entry of the interlocutory order (or denial of motion to vacate an ex parte order).","c":"R. 307(a), (b)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the interlocutory order)","note":""}},{"t":"Petition to review TRO ruling (and notice in circuit court)","d":"2 days from entry or denial of the TRO order.\nResponse due 2 days after petition; decision within 5 business days.","c":"R. 307(d)(1)","due":{"n":2,"from":"the trigger date (entry or denial of the TRO order)","note":"Response due 2 days after petition; decision within 5 business days."}},{"t":"Petition for leave to appeal interlocutory order (Rule 306)","d":"30 days from entry of the order.\nIncludes denial of Citizen Participation Act motion (306(a)(9)). Custody petitions under 306(a)(5): 14 days.","c":"R. 306(c)(1)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order)","note":"Includes denial of Citizen Participation Act motion (306(a)(9)). Custody petitions under 306(a)(5): 14 days."}},{"t":"Answer to Rule 306 petition","d":"21 days from filing of the petition.","c":"R. 306(c)(2)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the petition)","note":""}},{"t":"Application for leave to appeal certified question","d":"30 days from entry of order or trial court’s Rule 308 statement, whichever is later.\nAnswer due 21 days after the application’s due date.","c":"R. 308(b)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of order or trial court’s Rule 308 statement, whichever is later)","note":"Answer due 21 days after the application’s due date."}},{"t":"Bond extension limit in circuit court","d":"45 days from aggregate of circuit court extensions for filing appeal bond.\nUnless parties stipulate otherwise.","c":"R. 305(c)","due":{"n":45,"from":"the trigger date (aggregate of circuit court extensions for filing appeal bond)","note":"Unless parties stipulate otherwise."}},{"t":"Docketing statement (appeal as of right)","d":"14 days from filing of notice of appeal.\n7 days for Rule 307 appeals.","c":"R. 312(a)(1)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing of notice of appeal)","note":"7 days for Rule 307 appeals."}},{"t":"Record on appeal","d":"63 days from filing of the (last) notice of appeal.\nExtension motion before deadline or within 35 days after with reasonable excuse.","c":"R. 326","due":{"n":63,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the)","note":"Extension motion before deadline or within 35 days after with reasonable excuse."}},{"t":"Appellant’s brief","d":"35 days from filing of the record on appeal.","c":"R. 343(a)","due":{"n":35,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the record on appeal)","note":""}},{"t":"Appellee’s brief","d":"35 days from due date of appellant’s brief.","c":"R. 343(a)","due":{"n":35,"from":"the trigger date (due date of appellant’s brief)","note":""}},{"t":"Reply brief","d":"14 days from due date of appellee’s brief.","c":"R. 343(a)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (due date of appellee’s brief)","note":""}},{"t":"Interlocutory (Rule 307) appellant’s brief","d":"7 days from filing of the Rule 328 supporting record.\nAppellee 7 days later; reply 7 days after that.","c":"R. 307(c)","due":{"n":7,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the Rule 328 supporting record)","note":"Appellee 7 days later; reply 7 days after that."}},{"t":"Petition for rehearing","d":"21 days from filing of the reviewing court’s judgment.\nExtensions disfavored.","c":"R. 367(a)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the reviewing court’s judgment)","note":"Extensions disfavored."}},{"t":"Petition for leave to appeal to Supreme Court","d":"35 days from appellate Court judgment, or denial of timely rehearing petition / judgment on rehearing.\nExtensions only in the most extreme and compelling circumstances.","c":"R. 315(b)(1)","due":{"n":35,"from":"the trigger date (appellate Court judgment, or denial of timely rehearing petition / judgment on rehearing)","note":"Extensions only in the most extreme and compelling circumstances."}},{"t":"Answer to petition for leave to appeal","d":"21 days from expiration of the time to file the petition.\nOptional; no reply allowed.","c":"R. 315(f)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (expiration of the time to file the petition)","note":"Optional; no reply allowed."}},{"t":"Election to stand on PLA or file brief","d":"14 days from order allowing leave to appeal.\nAdditional brief due 35 days after leave allowed.","c":"R. 315(h)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (order allowing leave to appeal)","note":"Additional brief due 35 days after leave allowed."}},{"t":"Mandate issues (earliest)","d":"35 days from entry of judgment or order denying rehearing.","c":"R. 368(a)","due":{"n":35,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment or order denying rehearing)","note":""}},{"t":"Motion for original submission date after e-filing rejection","d":"5 days from notice of rejection (court days).\nCourt days, not calendar days.","c":"R. 9(f)(1)","due":{"n":5,"from":"the trigger date (notice of rejection)","note":"Court days, not calendar days."}}]]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/"]]},"rulesName":"Illinois Supreme Court Rules","rulesSrc":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"101","t":"Summons – form and issuance","s":"Summons issues under the court’s seal and must carry the statewide notice telling defendants to file an Appearance and Answer/Response and how to e-file. In money cases up to $50,000 (or arbitration cases with a local fixed date) the summons sets a return day 40–61 days after issuance; in eviction and replevin of personal property 7–40 days; when serving under 735 ILCS 5/2-208, 40–60 days with no default until 30 days after service. All other summonses require an appearance within 30 days after service; a waiver-of-service request under 2-213 must follow the Article II form, and use of the wrong summons form does not defeat jurisdiction.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"102","t":"Service of summons and complaint; return","s":"A 30-day summons cannot be served more than 30 days after its date; a return-day summons must be served at least 21 days (money cases) or 3 days (eviction/2-208 cases) before the appearance date. On a 735 ILCS 5/2-203.1 motion the court may order service by social media direct message, e-mail, or text if the defendant can receive it, with a supporting affidavit, prescribed message wording, a follow-up mailing within 10 days, and a proof of service with screenshots. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(m), there is no fixed outside deadline to complete service; diligence is policed under Rule 103(b).","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"102.1","t":"Service in protective-order proceedings","s":"A respondent who appears remotely in a protective-order case is personally served, and is ‘present’ when the order issues, if the order’s operative terms are read in open court; the clerk records the service and sends a copy.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"103","t":"Alias summons; dismissal for lack of diligence","s":"The clerk issues alias summonses on request. If the plaintiff fails to use reasonable diligence to serve a defendant before limitations runs, the action may be dismissed without prejudice; if the lack of diligence occurs after limitations has run, the dismissal is with prejudice as to that defendant (but does not bar vicarious-liability claims against others). The court looks at the totality, including diligence in any earlier dismissed case and in a case refiled under 13-217.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"104","t":"Service of pleadings and papers; filing","s":"Every summons must have the complaint attached. Later pleadings, motions, and documents are filed with a certificate or proof of service on all parties who have appeared and are not in default. Failure to serve does not affect jurisdiction; the offending party pays the cost of the aggrieved party getting the document from the clerk.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"105","t":"New or additional relief against defaulted parties – notice","s":"A party in default must get notice, with the new pleading attached, of any new or additional relief sought, warning that a default may be taken unless the party appears within 30 days after service, receipt of certified mail, or first publication. Notice may be served by any summons method, by certified or registered mail (restricted delivery for individuals, complete on receipt), or by publication where publication is otherwise allowed.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"106","t":"Notice of petitions under 2-1401, 2-1601, and 12-183(g)","s":"Petitions for relief from or revival of judgments are noticed by the Rule 105 methods, not by ordinary service on counsel.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"107","t":"Notice of hearing for an order of replevin","s":"Notice of a replevin hearing follows the Article II form and must be served at least 5 days before the hearing, by summons methods or by Rule 284 certified mail.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"108","t":"Notice to heirs and legatees on admission or denial of a will","s":"Notices under section 6-10 of the Probate Act must include the prescribed explanation-of-rights forms (Forms 1–4) when a will, including a foreign will proved by copy, is admitted or denied.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"109","t":"Reserved","s":"Reserved; no rule text.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"110","t":"Explanation of rights in independent administration","s":"Notices under the Probate Act when independent administration is granted must enclose the prescribed explanation of rights and petition-to-terminate form.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"113","t":"Mortgage foreclosure practice","s":"For foreclosures filed on or after May 1, 2013, the note with all indorsements and allonges must be attached to the complaint, and any default or foreclosure-judgment motion needs a detailed prove-up affidavit (affiant’s basis, records reviewed, software used). Plaintiff’s counsel prepares a notice of default within 2 business days after default for the clerk to mail; notice of sale goes out at least 10 business days before the sale; surplus funds trigger a special notice; the court appoints a special representative for a deceased mortgagor with no estate.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"114","t":"Loss mitigation affidavit","s":"Where a mortgagor has appeared, the plaintiff must comply with any applicable loss-mitigation program and file an affidavit describing it before or when moving for a foreclosure judgment; the court may stay or deny judgment for noncompliance.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"131","t":"Form of documents","s":"Documents must be legible and captioned in the court and cause, plaintiff first. Attorneys must show name, business address, telephone, and e-mail, designating one primary and up to two secondary e-mail addresses; self-represented litigants with e-mail must designate one address for Rule 11 service.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"132","t":"Designation of cases","s":"The initiating document’s caption must state the division (‘at law,’ ‘in chancery,’ ‘small claim,’ etc.); a wrong designation does not affect jurisdiction.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"133","t":"Pleading statutory duty, judgments, conditions precedent","s":"Cite the statute when alleging breach of a statutory duty; a judgment may be pleaded generally by date and nature; performance of conditions precedent may be alleged generally, but a denial must state the facts of nonperformance.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"134","t":"Incorporation by reference","s":"Facts adequately stated once may be incorporated by reference elsewhere in the pleading or in other pleadings.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"135","t":"Pleading equitable matters","s":"Equitable matters may be pleaded as a single equitable cause of action without counts; when legal and equitable actions are joined and treated as separate, plead them in counts marked ‘separate action at law’ and ‘separate action in chancery.’","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"136","t":"Denials","s":"A pleader may deny all allegations of a paragraph (or all not specifically admitted) without restating them. Unless ordered, no response to a reply is required and new matter in a reply is deemed denied.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"137","t":"Signing of pleadings; sanctions","s":"An attorney’s signature certifies reasonable inquiry, legal warrant, and proper purpose, as in Fed. R. Civ. P. 11, and pleadings need not be verified unless a rule or statute says so. Unlike Rule 11 there is no 21-day safe harbor in the text; a sanctions motion must be filed within 30 days after final judgment (or after ruling on a timely post-judgment motion) in the same case, and the judge must explain any sanction in writing. An attorney may ghostwrite or review a self-represented party’s filing without appearing and may rely on the party’s facts unless known to be false.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"138","t":"Personal identity information","s":"Do not file full Social Security or taxpayer numbers, driver’s license, financial account, or debit/credit card numbers; only the last four digits are allowed. When full numbers are required, file them in a separate ‘Notice of Confidential Information Within Court Filing,’ which is impounded. Anyone may move to redact a noncompliant filing, and willful violations can draw fees.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"139","t":"Eviction practice","s":"For eviction cases filed on or after July 17, 2020, the complaint must attach the eviction notice or demand with proof of service (or a form affidavit), and, for actions on a written lease under 9-210, the lease or relevant portions (or a form affidavit).","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"181","t":"Appearances, answers, and motions – timing","s":"On a 30-day summons, the 30 days run from the day the summons is left with the person served (not any later mailing); appearing by motion within that time extends the answer date to whatever the court sets. On a return-day summons in a money case, a defendant who appears in writing without answering gets 10 days after the return day to answer or move. In eviction cases no answer is needed unless ordered, and the complaint is deemed denied; small claims follow Rule 286.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"182","t":"Time for replies, answers to counterclaims, and other motions","s":"Replies are due 21 days after the last day allowed for the answer; answers or motions to counterclaims are due 21 days after the last day allowed for the counterclaim; a motion attacking any pleading other than the complaint is due 21 days after the last day allowed for that pleading.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"183","t":"Extensions of time","s":"For good cause shown on motion with notice, the court may extend any rule deadline, before or after it expires.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"184","t":"Hearings on motions","s":"A rule or statutory deadline for filing a motion does not require the motion to be heard within that time; either party may call it up before or after the filing period.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"187","t":"Forum non conveniens motions","s":"A motion to dismiss or transfer for forum non conveniens must be filed no later than 90 days after the last day allowed for the movant’s answer, and hearings should allow time for discovery on the facts. Dismissal is conditioned on the defendant accepting service and waiving limitations if the plaintiff refiles in the other forum within 6 months; if the other forum refuses jurisdiction, the plaintiff may reinstate within 30 days.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"191","t":"Affidavits for summary judgment, 2-619, and 2-301 motions","s":"Summary judgment (2-1005) and 2-619 motions must be filed before any dispositive-motion cutoff. Supporting and opposing affidavits, and affidavits on a 2-301 jurisdiction motion, must be on personal knowledge, state specific admissible facts (not conclusions), attach sworn or certified copies of documents relied on, and show the affiant is competent. Rule 191(b) lets a party who cannot get key affidavits name the witnesses and ask for a continuance or discovery, the Illinois counterpart to Fed. R. Civ. P. 56(d).","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"192","t":"Summary judgment on fewer than all issues","s":"When summary judgment does not resolve the whole case, the court may postpone entry of judgment, enter it, or enter it and stay enforcement; a smaller cross-demand may be netted against the movant’s demand.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"201","t":"General discovery provisions","s":"Discovery reaches ‘full disclosure’ of any matter relevant to the subject matter of the action, a broader test than the federal claim-or-defense-and-proportionality standard, though proportionality is a factor for protective orders (201(c)(3)). Work product is protected only for counsel’s theories, mental impressions, and litigation plans; consultants are discoverable only on exceptional circumstances. No discovery before all defendants have appeared or are due to appear without leave; every discovery motion needs a statement of personal consultation (201(k)); discovery is not filed; privilege claims need a description (201(n)); clawback of inadvertently produced privileged material is in 201(p); discovery is limited to jurisdiction while a 2-301 motion is pending (201(l)).","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"202","t":"Purposes of depositions","s":"Every deposition notice must say whether it is a discovery or an evidence deposition; if it does not, it is a discovery deposition only. Discovery and evidence depositions of the same witness are taken separately unless stipulated or ordered, and no discovery deposition is allowed if an evidence deposition is taken within 21 days of trial.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"203","t":"Where depositions may be taken","s":"Unless agreed, in the county where the deponent resides, works, or does business (a plaintiff in the county of suit), but the court may order a party or its officer or employee to appear elsewhere. A remote deposition is deemed taken where the deponent is.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"204","t":"Compelling a deponent’s appearance","s":"Subpoenas are issued by the clerk or by an attorney admitted in Illinois who is counsel of record; mail service is proved by return receipt at least 7 days before appearance with fee and mileage enclosed. Notice alone compels a party or its current officer, director, or employee. A records-only deposition requires serving copies on all parties at least 3 days before, and the subpoena must be attached to the notice and filed at least 14 days before. Nonparty treating physicians may be deposed only by agreement plus consent, or by court-ordered subpoena; foreign-case subpoenas under the Uniform Interstate Depositions and Discovery Act follow Rule 17.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"205","t":"Persons before whom depositions are taken","s":"Depositions are taken before an officer authorized to administer oaths (or a U.S. consular officer abroad) or a court appointee; commissions and letters rogatory are optional; no relative, attorney, or financially interested person may preside.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"206","t":"Method of taking oral depositions","s":"Notice must give reasonable advance warning, state discovery or evidence purpose, and disclose any audio-visual recording; an entity may be named with topics and must designate witnesses (as in Fed. R. Civ. P. 30(b)(6)). A discovery deposition is limited to 3 hours (the federal limit is 7) absent stipulation or court order; objections must be concise; no depositions on weekends or court holidays without agreement; remote depositions are allowed by notice, with technology time not counted against the limit.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"207","t":"Signing and filing depositions","s":"Unless signature is waived, the deponent may review the transcript and correct only reporting or transcription errors, not substance; if the deponent does not review within 28 days after notice, the deposition may be used as if signed. Transcripts are not filed as a matter of course.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"208","t":"Fees and charges; copies","s":"The party taking the deposition pays the witness, officer, and attendance charges; the party ordering transcription pays for it; the court may tax these as costs. Each party pays its own Rule 213(f)(3) controlled expert’s fees unless manifest injustice would result.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"209","t":"Failure to attend or to serve subpoena","s":"A party who notices a deposition and then fails to attend, or fails to subpoena the deponent, may be ordered to pay the expenses and fees of a party who showed up; remote attendance counts.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"210","t":"Depositions on written questions","s":"Cross-questions are due within 14 days after service of the direct questions, redirect within 7 days, and re-cross within 7 days; the officer takes the answers with no party or lawyer present.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"211","t":"Errors and irregularities in depositions","s":"Objections to the notice must be promptly served in writing; objections to the officer’s qualification must be made before or promptly after discovery; objections to form and curable errors are waived unless made at the deposition; objections to the form of written questions are due within the time for the next round (7 days for the last).","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"212","t":"Use of depositions","s":"A discovery deposition may be used only to impeach, as a party admission, under a hearsay exception, as an affidavit, or, with notice, as evidence if the deponent (not a controlled expert) is dead or infirm and no evidence deposition was taken. An evidence deposition of a physician may be used regardless of availability; other evidence depositions may be used if the witness is dead, ill, imprisoned, out of the county, or unobtainable by subpoena. Depositions from a dismissed earlier case or another U.S. jurisdiction between the same parties may be used.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"213","t":"Interrogatories; witness disclosure","s":"No more than 30 interrogatories including subparts (the federal limit is 25), answered under oath within 28 days. Rule 213(f) requires disclosure of lay witnesses, independent experts, and controlled experts (with opinions, bases, qualifications, and reports) on interrogatory, and 213(g) limits direct testimony at trial to what was disclosed in answers or a discovery deposition. There is a continuing duty to supplement.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"214","t":"Production of documents and inspection","s":"Requests may seek documents, ESI, tangible things, or entry onto land, with a response time of at least 28 days (federal: 30). The responding party must produce or object, may object on proportionality grounds, and must serve an affidavit stating whether production is complete; a party claiming the items are not in its control may be examined in court or by deposition. Duty to supplement continues.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"215","t":"Physical and mental examinations","s":"On motion when a condition is in controversy, the court may order an examination by a licensed professional, with the requesting party paying the examiner, lost earnings, and expenses. The examiner’s report must be delivered within 21 days after the exam or the examiner’s findings and testimony are excluded (except at the examinee’s instance). The court may also order an impartial examination by a panel physician chosen by the Administrative Office of the Illinois Courts.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"216","t":"Requests to admit","s":"Each fact or document’s genuineness is admitted unless within 28 days the party serves a sworn denial or detailed reasons, or written objections. Limit of 30 requests, each subpart counted, served in a separate document bearing a boldface 12-point warning that failure to respond within 28 days means the facts are deemed true. Admissions bind only in the pending case and any 13-217 refiling; a public-records notice is deemed admitted unless inaccuracy is shown by affidavit within 28 days.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"217","t":"Depositions to perpetuate testimony","s":"Before suit, a verified petition (with notice served at least 21 days before the hearing) may obtain an order to perpetuate testimony; while an appeal is pending or the time to appeal runs, the trial court may allow perpetuation depositions for possible further proceedings.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"218","t":"Pretrial procedure and case management","s":"Unless a local rule approved by the Supreme Court provides otherwise, the court must hold an initial case management conference within 35 days after the parties are at issue and no later than 182 days after the complaint is filed. The resulting order controls the case, and disclosure and discovery deadlines must be set so discovery closes at least 60 days before the anticipated trial date unless the parties agree otherwise.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"219","t":"Discovery sanctions","s":"Fees are mandatory on a motion to compel unless the refusal (or the motion) was substantially justified; a party who denies a request to admit that is later proved pays the cost of proof unless the denial had good reason. Sanctions for unreasonable noncompliance include stays, debarring pleadings or claims, barring witnesses, default or dismissal, judgment interest for pretrial delay, fees, and monetary penalties for willful misconduct, with written reasons required. Under 219(e) a voluntary dismissal does not escape discovery deadlines and orders, and the court may make the dismissing party pay the opponent’s defense expenses.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"222","t":"Limited and simplified discovery (cases up to $50,000)","s":"Applies to mandatory-arbitration cases and money-damages claims up to $50,000 (not small claims, family, or equitable cases); every money-damages complaint must attach an affidavit stating whether damages sought exceed $50,000, and any judgment above that is reduced to $50,000. Absent a local rule, parties must make detailed written initial disclosures within 120 days after the responsive pleading, with an affidavit of completeness; undisclosed evidence is excluded at trial. Discovery is limited to 30 interrogatories, 3-hour depositions of parties and of treating physicians and experts identified as trial witnesses, evidence depositions only with leave, and no Rule 216 requests within 60 days before trial without leave.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"223","t":"Reserved","s":"Reserved; no rule text.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"224","t":"Pre-suit discovery to identify responsible persons","s":"A verified petition in an independent action may seek discovery solely to identify persons who may be liable in damages, served with a special summons. The order is limited to identification, expires automatically 60 days after issuance unless extended, and the petitioner pays the respondent’s reasonable compliance costs. There is no federal counterpart.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"231","t":"Motions for continuance","s":"A continuance for absent evidence requires an affidavit showing diligence, the specific facts, the witness’s residence, and that the evidence can be obtained; it is denied if the opponent will admit the affidavit as the witness’s testimony. Military service in wartime and General Assembly membership during session are grounds; a continuance sought after the case is reached for trial needs a sufficient excuse.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"232","t":"Trial of equitable and legal matters","s":"A single equitable cause is tried as in equity. Where legal and equitable matters are joined, the court decides severability, and properly demanded legal issues go to the jury while equitable issues are heard by the court.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"233","t":"Order of proceeding","s":"Parties proceed at every stage (jury selection, openings, evidence, closings) in the order they appear in the pleadings unless agreed or ordered.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"234","t":"Voir dire","s":"The judge conducts voir dire and must permit the parties to supplement it with direct questioning for a reasonable time; questions may not concern law or instructions.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"235","t":"Opening statements","s":"The plaintiff may open as soon as the jury is impaneled and the defendant immediately after; any other timing is discretionary.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"236","t":"Business records","s":"Records made in the regular course of business at or near the time are admissible, with lack of personal knowledge going to weight; police accident reports are expressly excluded from this rule.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"237","t":"Compelling witnesses at trial","s":"Subpoenaed witnesses must appear if fees and mileage are tendered (mail service proved by receipt at least 7 days before). A party, or its current officer, director, or employee, can be required to appear at trial (in person or remotely) and bring original documents simply by serving a Rule 237(b) notice on the party; noncompliance can draw any Rule 219(c) sanction.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"238","t":"Impeachment; hostile witnesses","s":"Any party may impeach a witness, including its own; a hostile or unwilling witness may be examined as if on cross.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"239","t":"Jury instructions","s":"Illinois Pattern Jury Instructions (IPI) must be used when applicable unless the court finds the IPI inaccurate; non-IPI instructions must be simple, brief, and impartial. Each tendered instruction must be marked ‘IPI No. __,’ ‘IPI No. __ Modified,’ or ‘Not in IPI’; objections must be specific; the court may instruct before openings and must instruct after closings, and jurors may not get written copies before closing argument ends.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"240","t":"Directed verdicts","s":"An order directing a verdict takes effect without the jury’s assent.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"241","t":"Remote testimony in civil trials and evidentiary hearings","s":"The judge may allow testimony by video conference for good cause with safeguards (telephone only in compelling circumstances) and may allow nontestifying participants to appear remotely; arbitrators may do so by agreement. The judge may allocate costs so they are not a barrier to access.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"242","t":"Remote jury selection","s":"Remote jury selection by video is allowed with all parties’ consent, or without consent if the judge finds a compelling circumstance, consistent with constitutional rights and public access.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"243","t":"Juror questions to witnesses","s":"The court may let jurors submit written questions after counsel’s questioning; counsel may object outside the jury’s presence, the court asks approved questions, and counsel get limited follow-up. Rule 213(g) disclosure limits apply to juror questions.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"271","t":"Orders on motions","s":"Unless the court directs otherwise, the prevailing party’s attorney prepares and presents the order; orders may be prepared and signed electronically.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"272","t":"When judgment is entered","s":"If the judge requires a written judgment to be signed (or a local rule requires a draft order), the judgment is final only when the signed judgment is filed; otherwise it is entered when entered of record. This date starts the appeal clock.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"273","t":"Effect of involuntary dismissal","s":"Unless the order or a statute says otherwise, an involuntary dismissal (other than for lack of jurisdiction, improper venue, or failure to join an indispensable party) is an adjudication on the merits.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"274","t":"One postjudgment motion","s":"A party may file only one postjudgment motion directed at a final judgment, within 30 days or any extension; a new or modified judgment allows one motion directed at it. Each timely motion tolls finality and appealability until decided. A pending Rule 137 claim does not extend the time for postjudgment motions but may delay appealability under Rule 303(a)(1).","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"275","t":"Reserved","s":"Reserved; no rule text.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"276","t":"Opening a judgment by confession","s":"The motion must be supported by a Rule 191 affidavit and a verified proposed answer; if a prima facie meritorious defense and diligence are shown, the judgment is opened (in whole or part) and the original judgment stands as security. Trial is to the court unless a jury is demanded and paid for when the opening order is entered.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"277","t":"Citation to discover assets","s":"A citation proceeding under 735 ILCS 5/2-1402 may start immediately after a money judgment, in the court of entry or any court where the judgment is filed, against the debtor or any third party; examination is at least 5 days after service, and written interrogatory answers from a third party are due within 3 days of service. A later citation against the debtor needs leave of court on affidavit. The proceeding and its lien end automatically 6 months after the respondent’s first appearance unless extended; a citation served before a timely postjudgment motion is not dismissed. Rewritten in 2026.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"278","t":"Exemption claims in enforcement proceedings","s":"A judgment debtor may claim statutory exemptions orally on the return date or by an expedited motion that is granted automatically unless the creditor objects within 5 business days (otherwise a hearing within 7 business days). Agreed orders must be ruled on within 2 business days; exemptions are liberally construed, may be claimed repeatedly, and the 12-1001(b) wildcard applies collectively across accounts.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"279","t":"Reserved","s":"Reserved; no rule text.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"280","t":"Credit card and debt buyer actions – applicability","s":"Part I applies to any complaint with a claim originating from a credit card or by a debt buyer collecting a consumer debt.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"280.1","t":"Definitions","s":"Defines affidavit, assignment, charge-off balance and date, consumer debt, credit card, debt buyer, payment, principal, and related terms for Part I.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"280.2","t":"Complaint requirements","s":"The complaint must print the signer’s name, attach the Supreme Court’s Credit Card or Debt Buyer Collection Affidavit signed by the plaintiff or its agent (not plaintiff’s attorney), attach the contract or an account document (a charge-off statement suffices for revolving credit), and state that suit is within the limitations period.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"280.3","t":"Continuance or voluntary dismissal on trial date","s":"Absent a properly noticed written motion, a trial-day motion to continue or voluntarily dismiss is denied and the case is tried, unless all parties knowingly consent and justice is served or the court cannot proceed.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"280.4","t":"Consequences of noncompliance","s":"If the plaintiff does not comply with Part I, no default judgment may be entered and the complaint may be dismissed.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"280.5","t":"Identity theft defense","s":"A defendant claiming identity theft serves the Attorney General’s and the Supreme Court’s identity-theft affidavits; within 90 days the case must be dismissed unless the plaintiff files an affidavit showing the defendant is responsible.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"281","t":"Definition of small claim","s":"A small claim is a tort or contract action for money not over $10,000, exclusive of interest and costs.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"282","t":"Small claims – commencement; corporations","s":"A short, simple complaint with names, addresses, and the nature and amount of the claim (attaching any written instrument) starts the case. A corporation may sue in small claims only through counsel, but may defend through an officer, director, manager, or supervisor.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"283","t":"Small claims summons","s":"The summons sets a return day 40–61 days after issuance and follows the Rule 101(b) form.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"284","t":"Small claims service by certified or registered mail","s":"Unless a circuit rule says otherwise, the plaintiff may pay the clerk $2 plus postage to mail the summons and complaint, restricted delivery for individuals; a receipt showing delivery at least 21 days before the return day is proof of service.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"285","t":"Small claims jury demands","s":"Trial is to the court unless the plaintiff demands a jury when filing or the defendant demands one by the appearance date. The jury is 6 unless a party demands 12; fees are $12.50 for 6 and $25 for 12.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"286","t":"Small claims appearance, answer, and informal hearings","s":"The defendant must attend the first court date in person or remotely but need not file a written appearance or answer unless ordered; the complaint is deemed denied. The court may hold an informal hearing, relaxing procedure and evidence rules except hearsay and privilege, and must explain its ruling.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"287","t":"Small claims discovery and motions","s":"No depositions, interrogatories, other discovery, or requests to admit without leave of court; no motions without leave except under 735 ILCS 5/2-619 and 2-1001 (substitution of judge).","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"288","t":"Installment payment of small claims judgments","s":"The court may order payment by a date or in installments and stay enforcement meanwhile, but installments may not run more than 3 years.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"289","t":"Service in confession or collection proceedings up to $10,000","s":"Process to confirm a confessed judgment or collect a judgment of $10,000 or less may be served by Rule 284 certified mail.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"290","t":"Reserved","s":"Reserved; no rule text.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"291","t":"Administrative Review Law proceedings","s":"The clerk serves the Article II-form summons by registered or certified mail and files a certificate within 5 days of mailing; defendants appear within 35 days after the summons date. The agency’s answer is the administrative record, which goes into the record on appeal unless reduced by stipulation or order.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"292","t":"Summons to review Workers’ Compensation Commission orders","s":"On a written request for review, the circuit clerk issues the Article II-form summons to the Commission and all parties in interest.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"293","t":"Jury trial in involuntary admission","s":"A requested jury trial must begin within 30 days; continuances may not exceed 15 days except at the respondent’s request.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"294","t":"Collaborative process lawyers","s":"A collaborative process lawyer (and the whole firm, without screening or waiver) is disqualified from representing a party before a tribunal in the related matter, and must withdraw if the process fails, except for procedural filings, approval of the agreement, or emergency protective orders.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"295","t":"Matters assignable to associate judges","s":"The chief judge (or a designated circuit judge) may assign associate judges any suitable matter, including felony trials.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"296","t":"Restraints in mental health proceedings","s":"A respondent in involuntary admission, medication, or judicial admission proceedings may not be restrained unless the court first finds manifest necessity at a separate hearing on the record; only the least restrictive restraint is allowed.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"297","t":"Reserved","s":"Reserved; no rule text.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"298","t":"Application for waiver of court fees","s":"Fee-waiver applications use the statewide form under 735 ILCS 5/5-105, cost nothing to file, and are decided on the papers; if information is incomplete the court may not deny outright but must allow amendment or a hearing within 30 days of filing. Clients of civil legal services providers or court-sponsored pro bono programs proceed without fees on counsel’s certification.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"},{"n":"299","t":"Compensation of appointed counsel for indigent parties","s":"Appointed counsel get a reasonable fee, at a local-rule rate of not less than $150 per hour, capped at $750 for misdemeanors and $10,000 for felonies and listed termination-of-parental-rights and commitment matters, unless the court finds exceptional circumstances and the chief judge approves in advance.","u":"https://www.illinoiscourts.gov/rules/supreme-court-rules/"}]});
